> [[T_smoke]] = Temperature of smoke as it reaches the ceiling (K);
> [[Qc]] = Convective heat release rate of the fire (kW);
> [[m_smoke]] = Mass flow of smoke as it reaches the ceiling (minus mass flow of smoke lost through openings) (kg/s);
> cp = Specific heat capacity of air at ambient temperature ({{ cp }} kJ/kg/K);
> T∞ = Ambient temperature ({{ ambient_k }}K).
It is assumed that no smoke is lost through any openings in the warehouses.
It should be noted that as per Section 7.2.2.4 of PD 7974-2[5], it can be assumed that at the upper limits the temperature of the smoke layer will converge with the temperature of the flames, resulting in a maximum temperature rise of 900°C above ambient. As such, all temperatures calculated which are above {{ max_temp_c }}°C are rounded down.
